# Remote calls

`howdy_remote` is an optional package for typed calls from one Howdy service
to another. You define a *procedure* once, serve it from the service that
owns the data, and call it from anywhere. The calls travel over Erlang
distribution between your own nodes, or over HTTP across a trust boundary,
and the call site is the same for both.

```toml
howdy_remote = { git = "https://github.com/mikeyjones/howdy.git", ref = "v2.1.1", path = "remote" }
```

## Procedures

A procedure is a name, a codec for its input and a codec for its output:

```gleam
import gleam/dynamic/decode
import gleam/json
import howdy/codec
import howdy/remote

pub type User {
  User(id: Int, name: String)
}

pub fn get_user() -> remote.Procedure(Int, User) {
  remote.procedure("users.get", input: codec.int(), output: user())
}

pub fn list_users() -> remote.Procedure(Nil, List(User)) {
  remote.procedure("users.list", input: codec.nil(), output: codec.list(user()))
}

pub fn user_to_json(user: User) -> json.Json {
  json.object([#("id", json.int(user.id)), #("name", json.string(user.name))])
}

fn user() -> codec.Codec(User) {
  codec.new(encode: user_to_json, decoder: {
    use id <- decode.field("id", decode.int)
    use name <- decode.field("name", decode.string)
    decode.success(User(id:, name:))
  })
}
```

Put these in a module both services import. A small package that both
depend on works best: the caller and the server then always agree on names
and types.

Codecs come from `howdy/codec` in the core package. `codec.int`, `float`,
`string`, `bool` and `nil` cover the simple cases, and `codec.list(of:)` and
`codec.optional(of:)` build on them. For your own types, `codec.new` pairs a
JSON encoder with a decoder. The same codecs work for
[caching](/docs/cache/overview).

The name is all that crosses the wire, so it must be unique among the
procedures a server handles. Prefix it with the service, as in
`"users.get"`.

Inputs and outputs always travel as JSON, and are decoded when they arrive,
even between Erlang nodes. Two services deployed at different times can
disagree about a type; decoding turns that into an error at the boundary,
not a value of the wrong shape that crashes something later.

## Serving

A handler takes the input and returns a
[`service.Result`](/docs/controllers/services), the same type controllers
use, so one service function can back both an HTTP route and a procedure:

```gleam
import howdy/remote
import howdy/service

pub fn find(id: Int) -> service.Result(User) {
  list.find(users, fn(user) { user.id == id })
  |> result.replace_error(service.NotFound("user not found"))
}

pub fn server() -> remote.Server {
  remote.server()
  |> remote.handle(users_api.get_user(), find)
  |> remote.handle(users_api.list_users(), fn(_) { Ok(users) })
}
```

Then offer it to the other nodes of the cluster, over HTTP, or both:

```gleam
// To every connected node, over Erlang distribution:
let assert Ok(_) = remote.start(server())

// Over HTTP, as one POST /rpc/<name> route per procedure:
howdy.new()
|> howdy.controller(remote.controller(server(), at: "/rpc", token: secret))
```

`remote.start` links the server to the process that calls it;
`remote.supervised(server)` gives a child specification for a supervisor
instead. Each call runs in a process of its own on the serving node, so a
slow or crashing handler only affects its own call. Several nodes can serve
the same procedure, and share the calls between them.

When a handler returns `service.Internal(detail)`, the detail is logged on
the serving node and never sent to the caller.

## Calling

```gleam
remote.call(target, users_api.get_user(), 42, timeout: 5000)
```

The target decides how the call travels. Nothing else at the call site
changes:

- `remote.cluster()`: any connected node that serves the procedure. This
  node goes first if it serves it; otherwise each call picks a node at
  random.
- `remote.node("users@10.0.0.5")`: one node, connected on first use.
- `remote.http("https://users.internal/rpc", token: secret)`: a server
  mounted with `remote.controller`.

The result is the output, or a `remote.Error` saying why there is none:

```gleam
case remote.call(users, users_api.get_user(), id, timeout: 5000) {
  Ok(user) -> todo
  Error(remote.Failed(service.NotFound(_))) -> todo  // the handler's own error
  Error(remote.NoHandler(_)) -> todo                 // nothing serves it
  Error(remote.Unavailable(_)) -> todo               // node down, HTTP failed
  Error(remote.Timeout) -> todo                      // it may still have run
  Error(remote.Crashed(_)) -> todo                   // the handler crashed
  Error(remote.BadResponse(_)) -> todo               // the output did not decode
  Error(remote.Refused) -> todo                      // HTTP token rejected
  Error(_) -> todo
}
```

A `Timeout` does not mean the handler did nothing. Make procedures that
change data safe to call twice, or check before retrying.

## From a controller

`remote.respond` answers a request like `service.respond`. The handler's own
errors pass through, so a `NotFound` on the users service is still a `404`
here. Every transport failure becomes a logged `500`:

```gleam
pub fn app(users: remote.Target) -> howdy.App {
  howdy.new()
  |> howdy.controller(
    controller.new("/users")
    |> controller.get("/:id", fn(ctx) {
      use id <- param.int(ctx, "id")
      remote.call(users, users_api.get_user(), id, timeout: 5000)
      |> remote.respond(ctx, users_api.user_to_json)
    }),
  )
}
```

Taking the target as an argument, as here, lets the same app call over
distribution in one deployment and over HTTP in another.

## More ways to call

- `remote.cast(target, procedure, input)` calls without waiting for, or
  learning about, the outcome.
- `remote.multicall(procedure, input, timeout:)` calls every node that
  serves a procedure, in parallel, and returns each node's name and result.
  Use it to clear caches everywhere, or collect statistics.
- `remote.providers(procedure)` lists the nodes serving a procedure, for
  health checks.

To run the services as a cluster, and to choose between distribution and
HTTP, see [Clusters and security](/docs/remote/clusters).

## Testing

Both halves are plain Gleam, so they can be tested in one VM. Start the
server in the test, and call it with `remote.cluster()`, which includes the
node the test runs on:

```gleam
pub fn get_user_test() {
  let assert Ok(_) = remote.start(users.server())
  let assert Ok(user) =
    remote.call(remote.cluster(), users_api.get_user(), 1, timeout: 1000)
  assert user.name == "Ada"
}
```

To test a controller that calls out, pass it a target and send it requests
with [`howdy/testing`](/docs/guides/testing).
